(40, 2, 'OSNjKbHKDDGE', 'banana varieties for start farming', NULL, NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-07-14 03:09:22', '2026-07-14 03:09:22'),
(41, 2, 'OSNjKbHKDDGE', NULL, NULL, 'For starting a banana farm, it\'s essential to choose the right varieties. Based on general agricultural practices, here are some key points to consider:\n* Planting time: Typically, the ideal time for planting banana is during the warmer months, but the exact time may vary depending on your location.\n* Varieties: There are several varieties of bananas to choose from, including local varieties like Malbhog, which is known for its suitability in certain regions.\n\nWhen selecting a banana variety, consider factors such as climate, soil type, and market demand. Some popular varieties for commercial farming include:\n* Cavendish: A widely cultivated variety, known for its high yield and long shelf life.\n* Gros Michel: A variety with a sweeter taste, but more susceptible to disease.\n* Lady Finger: A variety with a slender shape and sweet taste, often preferred in local markets.\n\nBefore making a final decision, research the specific growing conditions and market demand in your area to ensure you choose the most suitable banana variety for your farm. Additionally, consider factors like disease resistance, yield, and harvesting time to maximize your crop\'s potential.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-07-14 03:09:25', '2026-07-14 03:09:25'),
(42, 2, 'OSNjKbHKDDGE', 'high selling variety is which one?', NULL, NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-07-14 03:09:50', '2026-07-14 03:09:50'),
(43, 2, 'OSNjKbHKDDGE', NULL, NULL, 'To determine the high-selling variety, we need to consider the type of crop you\'re inquiring about. Since you haven\'t specified a particular crop, I\'ll provide general information. High-selling varieties often depend on factors like market demand, climate, and seasonal trends. \n\nSome popular and high-selling crop varieties include:\n* Bhindi (Lady Finger) - Pusa Sawani is a recommended variety for its high yield and disease resistance.\n* Cowpea - Pusa Barsati is known for its adaptability to different climates and high market demand.\n* Other factors like soil type, irrigation facilities, and pest management also play a crucial role in determining the best variety for your farm.\n\nIf you could provide more information about the specific crop you\'re interested in, I\'d be happy to offer more tailored advice on high-selling varieties and best practices for cultivation. This will help you make informed decisions and maximize your yields.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-07-14 03:09:52', '2026-07-14 03:09:52'),
(44, 2, '8vgaoEWXc50C', 'My paddy leaves are turning yellow with brown spots. What disease is this and how do I treat it?', 'chatbot/XxRB42d2z6WJ5My1rZlwkBJ4COBSy7Sj32x02aqB.jpg', NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-08-19 04:55:04', '2026-08-19 04:55:04'),
(45, 2, '8vgaoEWXc50C', NULL, NULL, '### Rice Cultivation & Harvesting Guidelines\n\n- **Harvesting Indicators**: Rice is ready for harvest when grains turn yellow/golden (about 80-85% of the panicles), and moisture content drops to 20-22%. Grain should be firm when squeezed.\n- **Spacing**: Space transplanted seedlings at 20 x 15 cm or 15 x 15 cm for optimal ventilation and sunlight.\n- **Seed Selection**: Use certified seed varieties (e.g. Bg 352, Ld 368) suited for your ecological zone.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-08-19 04:55:09', '2026-08-19 04:55:09'),
(46, 2, 'bEsm4dEwLDaY', 'My paddy crop is turning yellow. Is it ready for harvest?', 'chatbot/cNyKJLgCSjfHikrk1R0kTFBjxn9iLVmx2UeVGzPS.jpg', NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-08-19 04:59:57', '2026-08-19 04:59:57'),
(47, 2, 'bEsm4dEwLDaY', NULL, NULL, '### Rice Cultivation & Harvesting Guidelines\n\n- **Harvesting Indicators**: Rice is ready for harvest when grains turn yellow/golden (about 80-85% of the panicles), and moisture content drops to 20-22%. Grain should be firm when squeezed.\n- **Spacing**: Space transplanted seedlings at 20 x 15 cm or 15 x 15 cm for optimal ventilation and sunlight.\n- **Seed Selection**: Use certified seed varieties (e.g. Bg 352, Ld 368) suited for your ecological zone.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-08-19 05:00:02', '2026-08-19 05:00:02'),
(48, NULL, 'sess_QzDxpiPF', 'How to control caterpillar pest in cabbage?', NULL, NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:44:33', '2026-08-19 15:44:33'),
(49, NULL, 'sess_QzDxpiPF', NULL, NULL, '### Pest Control\n\nrecommended him to apply decis @ 2ml per liter of water.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:44:36', '2026-08-19 15:44:36'),
(50, NULL, 'sess_MdM5IXDl', 'How to control caterpillar pest in cabbage?', NULL, NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:47:56', '2026-08-19 15:47:56'),
(51, NULL, 'sess_MdM5IXDl', NULL, NULL, '**Integrated control of cabbage caterpillars**\n\n- **Monitoring & early detection** – Scout fields daily once seedlings appear. Look for eggs (tiny yellow‑white clusters) on leaf undersides and young larvae feeding on leaf margins. When infestation reaches 5 % plant damage, start control measures.  \n- **Cultural & mechanical tactics** – Remove weeds and crop residues that shelter pupae. Rotate cabbage with non‑cruciferous crops (e.g., legumes) for at least 2 years. Hand‑pick large larvae in the early morning and destroy them; use light traps or sticky boards around field edges to catch adult moths.  \n\n- **Biological options** – Release or conserve natural enemies such as *Trichogramma* wasps (egg parasitoids) and *Bacillus thuringiensis* (Bt) kurstaki formulations, which are safe for humans and beneficial insects. Apply Bt at the first sign of feeding, repeating every 7‑10 days until larvae disappear.  \n- **Chemical safeguards (when needed)** – If damage exceeds economic thresholds, spray a low‑toxicity insecticide (e.g., neem oil, spinosad, or a pyrethroid labeled for cabbage) early in the morning or late evening to protect pollinators. Rotate active ingredients to delay resistance and follow the label’s pre‑harvest interval.  \n\n- **Post‑harvest hygiene** – After cutting, burn or deep‑bury plant debris to destroy pupae that may overwinter, reducing the next season’s pressure. Maintaining a clean field edge and proper irrigation (avoid excess moisture that favors pest buildup) rounds out an effective, sustainable management program.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:48:00', '2026-08-19 15:48:00'),
(52, NULL, 'sess_Jp6gXcRP', 'How to control caterpillar pest in cabbage?', NULL, NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:48:46', '2026-08-19 15:48:46'),
(53, NULL, 'sess_Jp6gXcRP', NULL, NULL, '**Caterpillar Management in Cabbage**\n\n- **Cultural Controls**  \n  • Rotate cabbage with non‑host crops (e.g., legumes, cereals) each season to break the pest cycle.  \n  • Remove and destroy crop residues after harvest; caterpillars overwinter in plant debris.  \n  • Plant early‑maturing varieties and stagger planting dates to avoid a uniform, abundant food source.\n\n- **Mechanical & Physical Measures**  \n  • Hand‑pick visible caterpillars in the early morning or late evening and drop them into soapy water.  \n  • Use row covers or fine mesh netting (≥1 mm mesh) to exclude adult moths from laying eggs.  \n  • Apply a light mulch of straw or shredded leaves; it can deter adult oviposition and make larvae movement harder.\n\n- **Chemical & Biocontrol Options**  \n  • Apply Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) kurstaki formulation at the first sign of infestation; re‑apply every 7–10 days while larvae are present.  \n  • If needed, use a low‑toxicity pyrethroid (e.g., deltamethrin) at the label‑recommended rate, targeting early‑instar larvae and observing pre‑harvest intervals.  \n  • Encourage natural enemies such as parasitic wasps (Trichogramma spp.) and predatory beetles by planting flowering borders (e.g., coriander, dill).  \n\nImplementing a combination of these tactics—starting with cultural and mechanical steps, then adding Bt or targeted insecticides when thresholds are exceeded—will keep caterpillar populations below damaging levels while preserving beneficial insects.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:48:49', '2026-08-19 15:48:49'),
(54, NULL, 'sess_xJsdmaiC', 'How to control caterpillar pest in cabbage?', NULL, NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:58:22', '2026-08-19 15:58:22'),
(55, NULL, 'sess_xJsdmaiC', NULL, NULL, '**Caterpillar control in cabbage – quick steps**\n\n- **Cultural & mechanical measures**  \n  • Rotate crops; avoid planting cabbage or other brassicas in the same bed for at least 2 years.  \n  • Remove and destroy any infested leaves or whole plants early to break the life cycle.  \n  • Use row covers or fine mesh nets (≥1 mm) to exclude adult moths from laying eggs.\n\n- **Biological options**  \n  • Release or encourage natural enemies such as *Trichogramma* wasps, parasitic flies (*Sturmiopsis*) and predatory beetles.  \n  • Apply *Bacillus thuringiensis* (Bt) kurstaki formulation at the first sign of larvae – spray every 7‑10 days until larvae disappear.  \n\n- **Chemical & safe‑use tips**  \n  • If infestation is heavy, use a low‑toxicity insecticide (e.g., neem oil 5 ml L⁻¹ or spinosad 2 g L⁻¹) early in the morning or late evening.  \n  • Follow label rates, wear protective gear, and observe the pre‑harvest interval (usually 3‑5 days) before market.  \n\nMonitor plants daily, especially the undersides of leaves, and act at the first larval stage for the best results.', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-08-19 15:58:27', '2026-08-19 15:58:27'),
(56, 2, 'kQFZHzhk55qX', 'How to improve rice cultivation?', NULL, NULL, 'user', NULL, '2026-08-20 04:09:05', '2026-08-20 04:09:05'),
(57, 2, 'kQFZHzhk55qX', NULL, NULL, '**Improving Rice Cultivation – Key Practices**\n\n- **Site selection & land preparation**  \n  • Choose flat, low‑lying fields with good water‑holding capacity.  \n  • Level the field to avoid waterlogging or dry spots.  \n  • Plow and harrow to a fine tilth; incorporate organic matter (compost or well‑rotted farmyard manure) at 10‑15 t ha⁻¹ to improve soil structure and fertility.\n\n- **Variety choice & seed treatment**  \n  • Select a high‑yielding, disease‑resistant variety suited to your climate (e.g., IR64, MTU1010, or locally recommended hybrids).  \n  • Treat seeds with a certified fungicide (e.g., carbendazim 0.5 g kg⁻¹) and a seed‑ling inoculant if using nitrogen‑fixing bacteria, then pre‑soak for 12‑24 h before sowing.\n\n- **Water management**  \n  • Maintain a continuous shallow flood (5–10 cm) during vegetative growth; drain to 2–5 cm depth a week before heading to promote root aeration and reduce lodging.  \n  • After flowering, keep the field flooded until grain maturity to suppress weeds and limit pest pressure.\n\n- **Nutrient management**  \n  • Apply a basal dose of N‑P‑K (e.g., 60 kg N ha⁻¹, 30 kg P₂O₅ ha⁻¹, 20 kg K₂O ha⁻¹) at transplanting.  \n  • Split the remaining nitrogen: 30 % at tillering, 30 % at panicle initiation, and 10 % as top‑dress at grain filling. Use urea or coated N products for better efficiency.  \n  • Conduct a soil test every 2–3 years and adjust micronutrient (Zn, Fe, B) applications as needed.\n\n- **Crop protection**  \n  • Scout fields weekly for pests (stem borer, leaf folder) and diseases (blast, sheath rot).  \n  • Use integrated pest management: cultural controls (proper spacing, timely water drainage), biological agents (Trichogramma, Bacillus thuringiensis), and targeted chemical sprays only when economic thresholds are exceeded.\n\n- **Harvest & post‑harvest handling**  \n  • Harvest when grain moisture is 20‑22 % to balance yield and grain quality.  \n  • Dry promptly to ≤14 % moisture in the sun or a mechanical dryer, then store in ventilated, moisture‑proof bags to prevent mold and insect damage.\n\nImplementing these steps systematically will boost yields, improve grain quality, and reduce input costs in your rice paddies. Feel free to ask for details on any specific stage or local variety recommendations!', 'assistant', NULL, '2026-08-20 04:09:10', '2026-08-20 04:09:10');
